Gozar is a Firefox VPN client for Android, Windows, macOS, and Linux.

Android uses its existing Kotlin application and its shared core. Desktop has a native Flutter
Material 3 interface and a Go core. It does not use Kotlin, a JVM, a browser-based interface, or
an HTTP API for the interface. The Flutter process communicates with the Go process through a
private standard-input/output pipe. The interface supports Persian, English, and the system
language, and keeps the selected language between launches. Android also offers the language
picker on the sign-in screen and in Settings. Desktop registration and email verification
use native forms; supported Mozilla security checks and image challenges appear in the app. The
account password is not saved. Separate Firefox sessions can be added and switched inside the app;
switching, adding an account while connected, or signing out disconnects the VPN first. Signing out
removes the active session while leaving other saved sessions available from the sign-in screen.
Android stores session tokens in app-private preferences; desktop stores them in a local configuration
file rather than the OS keychain. These tokens are sensitive and are not encrypted by Gozar.

Android adds the GlobalSign E46 and R46 roots only for fastly-masque.net and its subdomains,
while continuing to verify certificate chains and server hostnames. Its HTTP/2 and HTTP/1.1
CONNECT paths and packet handling have loopback and in-memory tests. The user reports that a friend
successfully connected on the previously affected physical Android 13 phone after the certificate
fix. Device certificate stores and server chains vary, so this is not a guarantee for all devices.
The app does not keep its previous in-memory diagnostics or crash file.

On Android, the sign-in email button opens Android's system account chooser. It accepts only
selected account names that are valid email addresses; manual entry remains available. It does
not perform Google Sign-In or use Gozar's saved sessions as system accounts. On Linux the equivalent
button reads email-like identities from GNOME Online Accounts on explicit request, when that
service is available. Windows and macOS do not provide a verified universal, provider-independent
system email picker in this build, so manual entry is used there. The saved Gozar account switcher
is a separate feature on all platforms.

For a separate local Android test installation, build with `-PgozarDiagnostic`. This uses the
`com.gozar.diagnostic` package and a separate app data directory. Without the official signing
credentials, a local build is not an update to the official Gozar installation.

Desktop builds are archives containing the application and its runtime libraries; they are not
MSI, DMG, or DEB installers. The Windows build contains Wintun. The desktop tunnel configures a
TUN interface and uses a userspace network stack; it needs administrator privileges. On Windows,
the application requests elevation when it starts. On Linux and macOS, launch the application
with appropriate administrator privileges to use the tunnel. The new desktop app does not set
or automatically clear a system proxy. If an older version left a proxy behind, review the system
proxy settings manually rather than assuming that a proxy belongs to Gozar. Desktop DNS queries
use DNS over HTTPS through the authenticated Firefox proxy; unsupported UDP is refused so clients
can fall back to TCP when they support it. Windows route commands do not open console windows.

The desktop tunnel is experimental. It has not been verified in a live connection in this
revision. Firefox account sign-in and registration were checked with mock responses, not a real
account. The non-interactive security check was also probed without account credentials. Mozilla
may present an unsupported security challenge or refuse a request. IPv6 is not routed through
the tunnel and may bypass it. UDP other than DNS is not supported. Mozilla's proxy supports only
certain destination ports, including 80, 443, 8080, and 8443. Do not rely on this build for privacy
or as a complete device-wide VPN. The desktop interface provides language, appearance, account
switching, recent-server preference, a manual release check, donation, and an ordered SOCKS5/HTTP
proxy-chain editor. Proxy chains are used by the Go core when connecting; proxy authentication is
not supported. Desktop per-application and per-domain routing, a dedicated LAN-bypass control,
and the route map are not implemented. Existing OS LAN routes may still bypass the tunnel.

Planned desktop work, subject to feasibility and testing, includes application-based routing
on Windows and Linux first; domain rules, an explicit LAN-bypass option and a route view;
provider-supported system-email selection; investigation of OS-backed token storage; and
better recovery and connectivity diagnostics. These are not implemented today. macOS per-app
routing and any additional IPv6 or UDP coverage require separate investigation, and arbitrary
UDP or leak-free VPN coverage is not promised. See the release notes for details.

Linux build, from the repository root:

    cd desktop_flutter
    flutter pub get
    flutter build linux --release
    cd ../go-desktop
    CGO_ENABLED=0 go build -trimpath -ldflags='-s -w' -o ../desktop_flutter/build/linux/x64/release/bundle/gozar-core ./cmd/gozar

The desktop build needs Flutter 3.24.5, Go 1.24, and the platform's native build dependencies.
On Linux, the built app requires GTK 3 and a graphical desktop. A Flutter bundle contains
multiple files: keep the Go core, the executable, and the bundled data and libraries together.
macOS downloads use .tar.xz; extract them in Terminal with `tar -xJf ARCHIVE.tar.xz`.

